From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent: 993 - never persist a backhaul BSS association block

An association block on a *backhaul* BSS is written straight to UCI, so it
survives a restart and a cold power cycle. Nothing clears it on its own:
agent_init_ifaces_assoc_mode() reloads disallow_assoc from UCI on every start,
pushes it back into the driver and disconnects all STAs. hostapd then advertises
MBO "association disallowed", and every bSTA picks the BSS as the right one and
is then required to skip it:

  wlan2: 3: 76:04:1c:81:bc:2f ssid='MAP--BH' level=-13 freq=6135
  wlan2:    selected based on RSN IE
  wlan2:    skip - MBO association disallowed (reason 2)

No auth attempt ever reaches the AP, so from the outside this is
indistinguishable from an RF, key or range problem, while the agents see the AP
at -13 dBm with a matching PSK and SSID. A node that ends up in this state never
rejoins the mesh -- observed on a 3-node lab after the backhaul dropped, across
two cold power cycles, until disallow_assoc was cleared by hand.

The client path already treats this as strictly temporary: a Validity Period is
mandatory for Client Blocking / Timed Block, it arms restrict_timer, and the
block lives in runtime state only. The bBSS path takes none of that -- it does
not read validity_period, arms no timer, and additionally persists to UCI.

Keep the block runtime-only so a restart always recovers, and keep persisting
the *unblock* so a node already stuck by an older build clears itself on the
first allow. Warn on every bBSS block: the originator of the request that armed
it is not yet identified, and this makes the next occurrence visible in the log.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/assoc_ctrl.c
+++ b/src/assoc_ctrl.c
@@ -804,7 +804,18 @@
 
 		if (!wifi_set_ap_mbo_association_mode(ap->ifname, disallow)) {
 			ap->cfg->disallow_assoc = disallow;
-			uci_apply_bss_association_mode(ap->ifname, disallow);
+			/* 993: a bBSS block must not outlive the process.
+			 * Persisting it bricks the backhaul across reboots
+			 * with no way back, so keep it runtime-only. Still
+			 * persist the unblock, so a node stuck by an older
+			 * build clears itself on the first allow.
+			 */
+			if (!disallow)
+				uci_apply_bss_association_mode(ap->ifname, false);
+			else
+				agnt_warn(LOG_STEER,
+					  "993: bBSS %s assoc blocked (runtime only)\n",
+					  ap->ifname);
 		}
 
 		goto send_ack;
--- a/src/agent.c
+++ b/src/agent.c
@@ -1618,7 +1618,16 @@
 	ret = wifi_set_ap_mbo_association_mode(ap->ifname, !allowed);
 	if (!ret) {
 		ap->cfg->disallow_assoc = !allowed;
-		uci_apply_bss_association_mode(ap->ifname, !allowed);
+		/* 993: see assoc_ctrl.c -- never persist a block on a
+		 * backhaul BSS. The only way back is wifi_del_sta()
+		 * landing exactly on max_sta - 1, which a sudden peer
+		 * loss never delivers.
+		 */
+		if (allowed || !(ap->cfg->multi_ap & 0x01))
+			uci_apply_bss_association_mode(ap->ifname, !allowed);
+		else
+			warn("993: bBSS %s assoc blocked (runtime only), num_sta=%d max_sta=%d\n",
+			     ap->ifname, ap->num_sta, ap->max_sta);
 	}
 
 	return ret;
